course: dictionary
lesson: 14943
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. «Rein» is one of these words. Its first meaning is about a horse: the long straps that a rider holds to guide it.
explain: «She pulled gently on the reins, and the horse slowed to a walk.» The horse is guided by the reins, so they must be the straps. Now read this: «The new captain took the reins of the team.» There are no straps here. Take the reins means take charge, so the word has changed its meaning.
explain: Check by swapping in your meaning. Does «The new captain took charge of the team» still make sense? It does, so you have the right meaning. Always ask: does my meaning fit the words around it?
fact: A rein is a long strap fixed to a horse's bit, used to guide the horse.
fact: «Rein» can be a noun (a rein, reins) or a verb: «She reined in her horse» means she slowed it down.
fact: «Take the reins» means take charge, and «give free rein» means let someone do as they like.
fact: In the UK, reins are straps put on a toddler to keep them safe near busy roads.
exercise: «He pulled on the reins, and the horse stopped.» What are the reins? | Blankets that keep a horse warm | Sticks used to hit the ground | Ropes for climbing trees | *Long straps used to guide a horse
exercise: «The head teacher gave the new student free rein to choose the class project.» What does free rein mean? | Ask the student to leave | Stop the student from doing anything | *Let the student do as they like | Give the student a horse
exercise: «Mum put reins on the toddler near the busy road.» Why did she do this? | To carry the toddler's shopping | To make the toddler run faster | *To keep the toddler safe | To tie the toddler to a tree
test: «Mia reined in her bike at the corner.» What does reined in mean? | *Slowed down and stopped | Fell off the bike | Asked for a lift home | Rode faster and faster
test: «Sam took the reins of the school council.» What does took the reins mean? | Went home early | *Took charge | Bought a new horse | Dropped the rope
exam: «Keep a tight rein on the budget.» What does keep a tight rein on mean? | Lose it on purpose | *Control it closely | Ignore it completely | Spend as much money as possible
